::GEO_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which tool is used to transfer the length of a segment during a construction? {
=Compass #Correct. A compass holds a fixed opening so a segment length can be transferred accurately.
~Protractor #A protractor measures angles, but it is not the tool used to transfer a segment length in this construction.
~Calculator #A calculator does not construct or transfer geometric distances.
~Graphing grid #A grid may help organize a drawing, but it does not transfer a segment length with construction precision.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>After opening the compass to the length of segment AB, what should happen before marking the copy? {
~Change the compass opening to make the copy fit. #Changing the opening changes the length, so the copy may not match segment AB.
=Keep the compass opening fixed. #Correct. Keeping the compass opening fixed preserves the original segment length.
~Use the protractor to measure AB. #A protractor measures angles, not segment length.
~Estimate the length by sight. #Estimation by sight is not a precise construction method.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>When copying an angle, why are arcs drawn from the original vertex and the new vertex? {
~They make the drawing look more complete. #Arcs are not decorative; they provide construction evidence.
~They measure the angle with a protractor. #The construction uses compass arcs, not protractor measurement.
=They help transfer the original angle opening. #Correct. Matching arcs and compass distances place the copied angle accurately.
~They create an angle bisector. #Bisecting an angle is a different construction and is not the goal here.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement shows a correct segment-copy result? {
~CD is longer than AB #A copied segment should match the original length, not be longer.
=CD = AB #Correct. If segment CD is copied from segment AB, their lengths should be equal.
~CD is perpendicular to AB #Copying a segment does not require a perpendicular relationship.
~CD is an angle copy #CD names a segment, not an angle.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which mistake would make an angle copy unreliable? {
~Drawing an initial ray from the new vertex #An initial ray is needed as a starting side for the copied angle.
~Drawing an arc from the original angle vertex #This is part of the angle-copy construction.
~Using the same compass opening on the new angle #Using the same opening helps transfer the original angle opening.
=Placing the second ray by sight instead of using arc intersections #Correct. An angle copy should use arcs and compass distances, not estimation by appearance.
}
